    <h1>Who Captures the Value?</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Land, Infrastructure, and the Hidden Ledger</div>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0.2rem;">Ping Xu</p>
    <p style="margin-top:0;">June 2026 · Boston · GFI Flow Intelligence</p>

    <!-- Preface -->
    <h2>Preface</h2>
    <p>I read only two kinds of things: scores and texts. A musical score is densely compressed information. In a single measure, melody, harmony, rhythm, dynamics, fingering, pedaling — six different kinds of information packed into a few inches of space. You have to decode them in a split second and execute them with your body, all at once.</p>
    <p>This trained me to see the whole system. A fugue is not a sequence of notes. It is multiple voices running simultaneously. You cannot look only at the right hand or only at the left hand. You have to hear all the voices at once and track where every thread goes. Later, when I faced policy systems, administrative processes, and land regimes, I used the same ability.</p>
    <p>The old order will not die on its own. Someone has to dismantle it. I am not at war with anyone. I am just the one who started dismantling.</p>
    <p><strong>Why write this book?</strong> In early 2026, I published the GL Framework — a tool for measuring "administrative friction." It uses eight parameters to calculate the ledger that GDP cannot see: waiting, repetition, collapse, information asymmetry, psychological cost, opportunity cost. This book is an application of that framework to land regimes.</p>
    <p>This book is not written for experts. It is written for those who have been consumed by this system. For the hotel manager in Quincy who owns a car, pays for parking, put down three months of rent, downloaded the smart lock app, and still asks himself at night: "why am I so tired?"</p>
    <p><strong>Who captures the value?</strong> That is the question I ask. And the question you should begin to ask. No matter where you live — the UK, the US, Hong Kong, Taiwan, China, Boston, anywhere — you can ask it.</p>

    <!-- Chapter 1: Two Ledgers -->
    <h2>Chapter One: Two Ledgers</h2>
    <h3>1.1 Earth Overshoot Day</h3>
    <p>On July 24, 2025, humanity used up Earth's entire annual budget. The remaining five months were borrowed from future generations. The United States reached its overshoot day on March 13 — less than three months to use its full annual allocation. Germany on May 3. China on May 23. This is not a metaphor. This is an actual ledger.</p>
    <h3>1.2 GDP Only Counts Half</h3>
    <p>GDP records completed transactions. It does not record who waited, who re-filed, who gave up at a collapse point, whose psychological cost was consumed. Those costs do not disappear. They are shifted. They just are not on the ledger. Suppose a city decides to build a subway. Years later, the subway opens. Land prices rise. On GDP's ledger, this is a success story. But what GDP does not tell you: how many residents were displaced? How much business income did local shops lose? Of the land value increase, how much returned to the government versus how much was captured by private landowners? These are not "side effects." They are the true cost of the subway.</p>
    <h3>1.3 The Invisible Ledger Has a Name: Cost Shifting</h3>
    <p>When a system is unwilling or unable to bear a certain cost, it shifts that cost to someone else — usually to those with less power, those who cannot refuse. A factory pollutes a river. The cost is shifted to downstream residents. A company classifies employees as contractors. The cost is shifted to the social safety net. A government leaves administrative friction on citizens. The cost is shifted to those without the power to refuse. GDP only sees completed transactions. It does not see who bears the cost.</p>
    <h3>1.4 The Central Question of This Book</h3>
    <p>Of all the forms of cost shifting, land regimes may be the most important and the most overlooked. Why? Because the value created by infrastructure ultimately capitalizes into land. A subway, a high-speed rail, an industrial park — these public investments cause surrounding land values to soar. This increase has nothing to do with any effort by the landowner. It is purely created by public decisions. But the question is: who captures this increase?</p>
    <p>This book analyzes six regimes: the UK, the US, Hong Kong, Taiwan, China, and the Global Commons.</p>

    <!-- Chapter 2: The UK Ledger -->
    <h2>Chapter Two: The UK Ledger</h2>
    <h3>2.1 A Country Where Aristocrats Still Own the Land</h3>
    <p>Half of England's land is owned by less than 1% of the population — about 25,000 landowners. Most are aristocrats and the Crown. Aristocrats and gentry hold about 30% of England's land. Some families trace their holdings to the Norman Conquest in 1066 — nearly a thousand years. The Crown holds large landholdings through the Duchy of Lancaster (about 46,500 acres) and the Duchy of Cornwall (about 135,000 acres). By contrast, people who own their own homes collectively own only 5% of England's land. The Duke of Westminster is one of Britain's most famous landowners, with vast holdings in Mayfair and Belgravia spanning over three centuries.</p>
    <h3>2.2 The Explosion and Transfer of Land Value</h3>
    <p>Land itself is not wealth. The right to develop it is. Agricultural land without development permission in England is worth about £23,000 per hectare. Once it receives residential planning permission, its value can soar to £2.67 million per hectare — a 116-fold increase. This increase does not come from anything the farmer planted or any investment the landowner made. It comes from public decisions. But who captures this increase? Landowners and developers. The UK Parliament's 2025 inquiry into "land value capture" stated plainly: "This land value increase has nothing to do with any productive activity or investment by the landowner or developer. It is purely the result of public policy decisions. But the vast profits flow to landowners and developers."</p>
    <h3>2.3 Summary</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box"><strong>The UK's ledger in one sentence: the public bears the cost, aristocrats capture the value.</strong></div>

    <!-- Chapter 3: The US Ledger -->
    <h2>Chapter Three: The US Ledger</h2>
    <h3>3.1 From Aristocrats to Capitalists</h3>
    <p>The US land system originated in Britain but took a dramatically different path. Britain's system was designed for concentration; the US system for dispersion — to put land in the hands of ordinary people. With vast territory, no feudal aristocracy, and colonial governments needing to attract immigrants, the 1862 Homestead Act allowed any adult citizen to claim 160 acres of public land by living on and cultivating it for five years.</p>
    <h3>3.2 Westward Expansion and Suburbanization</h3>
    <p>Westward expansion put vast land into ordinary hands, creating the world's highest proportion of small landowners. But when the frontier closed, the institutional logic changed. The mid-20th century Interstate Highway System — one of the largest public works in human history — pushed suburbanization to its extreme. Land values along highways soared. Who captured this increase? Land speculators, developers, and capitalists who bought suburban land early.</p>
    <h3>3.3 Financialization of Real Estate</h3>
    <p>The emergence of Real Estate Investment Trusts further financialized land value. Retail investors can hold land assets indirectly through REITs and share in the appreciation. But this does not change the core problem: the value created by public investment still flows to private capital.</p>
    <h3>3.4 Summary</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box"><strong>The US's ledger in one sentence: the public bears the cost, capitalists capture the value.</strong></div>

    <!-- Chapter 4: Hong Kong Ledger -->
    <h2>Chapter Four: Hong Kong Ledger</h2>
    <h3>4.1 The System Britain Left Behind</h3>
    <p>Hong Kong's land system was established during British colonial rule. When the British arrived, they applied a different approach than in Britain itself. In Britain, land is concentrated in aristocrats and the Crown, and private ownership exists. In the colony, Britain did something completely different: <strong>all land belonged to the Crown, and private individuals could only hold使用权 (leasehold)</strong>. This was the origin of the leasehold system.</p>
    <p>In 1841, the British Crown issued a proclamation declaring all land on Hong Kong Island and the Kowloon Peninsula to be Crown land. For over 150 years thereafter, Hong Kong maintained this principle: <strong>land never belonged to private individuals; private individuals could only hold leases</strong>, with terms ranging from 75 to 999 years. In other words, on this land, <strong>no one has ever owned the land</strong>. Not the British. Not the Chinese. Everyone is a "tenant" — only the lease term differs.</p>
    <h3>4.2 Why the British Designed It This Way</h3>
    <p>The British adopted a "public land, lease-only" system in the colony not for the welfare of the colonial people, but for control. Land is the most fundamental tool of colonial rule. Whoever controls land controls everything. By holding land ownership, the British ensured: the colonial government had ultimate decision-making power, land revenue flowed to the colonial government, and no one could challenge the colonial authority's land rights. Land lease revenue became a crucial source of colonial government income. From very early on, Hong Kong was a colony that "supported itself by selling land." This fiscal model continues to this day.</p>
    <h3>4.3 After the Handover: China Did Not Change This System</h3>
    <p>In 1997, Hong Kong was returned to China. A critical question arose: China's land system is "urban land state-owned, rural land collectively owned," while Hong Kong's system is "all land government-owned, private individuals hold usage rights." The two are not identical, but the core logic is the same — <strong>land does not belong to private individuals</strong>.</p>
    <p>Therefore, China chose to continue Hong Kong's existing system. Article 7 of the Basic Law of Hong Kong states:</p>
    <blockquote>"The land and natural resources within the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region shall be owned by the State... The Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region shall be responsible for their management, use, development, lease, or grant to individuals, legal persons, or organizations."</blockquote>
    <p>The practical effect: <strong>the leasehold system left by Britain continued almost unchanged</strong>. The Hong Kong government remains the sole supplier. Land lease revenue remains a pillar of government finances. 99-year leases remain the norm. The only change: land ownership shifted from "British Crown" to "People's Republic of China." Thus, Hong Kong became a unique hybrid: <strong>British institutional framework + Chinese sovereignty structure</strong>.</p>
    <h3>4.4 Why Are Housing Prices the Highest in the World?</h3>
    <p>Hong Kong has repeatedly ranked as the world's least affordable housing market. The reasons are threefold: First, land supply is artificially controlled. Second, the land auction system drives up land prices. Third, oligarchic developers dominate. CK Asset Holdings, Sun Hung Kai Properties, Henderson Land Development, New World Development — these four major developers have long dominated Hong Kong's real estate market. The value created by public investment partially returns to government through the auction system, but the vast majority ultimately flows into developer profits.</p>
    <h3>4.5 Who Captures the Value?</h3>
    <p>To answer this book's central question in one sentence: the value created by public investment in Hong Kong is shared by <strong>the government (through land lease revenue) and the four major developers (through profits)</strong>. This structure was designed by the British. The Chinese continued it. No one changed it.</p>
    <p>Notably: Mainland China's land system is "state-owned land, government land leasing, value capture and reinvestment into infrastructure." Hong Kong's land system is "state-owned land, government land leasing, value flows into fiscal revenue and developer profits." They share the same starting point (land does not belong to private individuals) but have diverged — China formed a value capture loop; Hong Kong did not. This is not because Hong Kong could not. It is because the system Hong Kong inherited from Britain was never designed for "value returning to the public."</p>
    <h3>4.6 Summary</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box"><strong>Hong Kong's ledger in one sentence: the public bears the cost, the government (land revenue) and major developers share the value — a system left by Britain, unchanged by China.</strong></div>

    <!-- Chapter 5: Taiwan Ledger -->
    <h2>Chapter Five: Taiwan Ledger</h2>
    <h3>5.1 The First Person in the World to Raise the Question</h3>
    <p>To many, Taiwan's housing price problem is a problem of the last two decades. In fact, it has been discussed for over a century. When Sun Yat-sen proposed "Equalization of Land Rights," he was not actually concerned about housing prices. He was concerned about something else: <strong>why should value created by the public be monopolized by private individuals?</strong> This is exactly the central question of this book. So Sun Yat-sen proposed: <strong>land value increment to the public</strong>. This principle was later written into Article 143 of the Constitution of the Republic of China. The problem: raising an idea is easy. Realizing it is very difficult.</p>
    <h3>5.2 The Birth of the Land Value Increment Tax</h3>
    <p>Taiwan established a rare system: the Land Value Increment Tax. In theory, when land is sold, the government taxes the increment, recovering part of the value created by public investment. This system was designed to prevent landowners from wholly monopolizing the results of public investment. From an institutional design perspective, it was very advanced. However, there is a vast distance between theory and reality.</p>
    <h3>5.3 Why the Goal Was Not Achieved</h3>
    <p>Because institutions encounter politics. Politics encounters interests. Interests change institutions. Over decades, various preferential measures emerged: owner-occupied housing preferences, long-term holding preferences, land readjustment preferences, incentive provisions, tax reduction measures. The result: the law still exists, the idea still exists, but the actual recovery rate has continuously declined. Thus, a strange phenomenon emerged: the government verbally supports land value increment to the public, but the market continuously rewards land appreciation.</p>
    <h3>5.4 Who Creates Wealth from the MRT?</h3>
    <p>To understand Taiwan's land appreciation, the easiest observation is the MRT. An MRT line is announced — surrounding land begins to rise. Construction begins — another rise. The MRT opens — another rise. The landowner may have done nothing, yet the land price doubles or even multiplies. The public pays the cost. Taxpayers pay the cost. Residents endure noise and traffic disruption during construction. But who ultimately captures the increment? Most flows to landholders.</p>
    <h3>5.5 High-Speed Rail and Land Readjustment Zones</h3>
    <p>The high-speed rail case is even more obvious. Many HSR station surroundings were originally farmland. After the HSR announcement, land prices rapidly changed. Public construction creates enormous value, but the distribution is unequal — early holders gain the most; latecomers bear the highest costs. Thus, generational differences emerge: the parent generation accumulates assets through land; the child generation must buy the same assets at higher prices. The value has not disappeared — only transferred.</p>
    <h3>5.6 The Hsinchu Science Park's Other Ledger</h3>
    <p>The Hsinchu Science Park is another example. From a GDP perspective, it is extremely successful. The semiconductor industry rose. Exports increased. Wages rose. A technology cluster formed. But another ledger also exists: housing prices rise, rents rise, cost of living rises. Thus emerges what this book calls: Ghost GDP. GDP increases, but the cost of living also increases. Part of the gain is recorded. Part of the cost is not.</p>
    <h3>5.7 The Entry Fee for Youth</h3>
    <p>In the past, buying a house required hard work. Today, many young people need two things: first, hard work; second, parental assistance. When a society begins to see down payments relying on parents, gifts relying on parents, inheritance determining wealth, it means land appreciation has begun to change opportunity distribution. Success no longer depends entirely on ability, but on whether one already stands on the appreciation side.</p>
    <h3>5.8 Taiwan's Version of Ghost GDP</h3>
    <p>Many costs do not appear in statistics: time spent house hunting, queuing time, commuting time, high rent pressure, delayed marriage, delayed childbirth, foregoing childbirth, chronic anxiety. GDP does not record these. But society pays them every day. These costs do not disappear — they are merely shifted.</p>
    <h3>5.9 Summary of Taiwan's Ledger</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box"><strong>Taiwan's ledger in one sentence: the public bears the cost, private individuals capture most of the value — the principle of land value increment to the public was written into the constitution, but not into reality.</strong><br><br>Compared with the UK, Taiwan has no hereditary aristocracy. Compared with the US, Taiwan's land supply is more limited. Compared with Hong Kong, government control capacity is lower. Compared with China, land value capture capacity is weaker. Thus, a fifth model emerges: the public bears the cost, private individuals share most of the value, and younger generations bear increasingly high entry costs.</div>

    <!-- Chapter 6: China Ledger -->
    <h2>Chapter Six: China Ledger</h2>
    <h3>6.1 A Completely Different Starting Point</h3>
    <p>If the UK's land system is built on aristocratic ownership, and the US's on private ownership, then China's system is built on a different premise: <strong>land does not belong to private individuals</strong>. According to China's Constitution, urban land is state-owned, and rural/suburban land is collectively owned. What individuals can obtain is usage rights, not ownership. This difference changes the entire flow of value.</p>
    <h3>6.2 How a High-Speed Rail Line Changes the Ledger</h3>
    <p>Suppose the government builds a high-speed rail line. UK model: landowners profit. US model: developers profit. China model: the HSR is built, surrounding land appreciates, the government leases usage rights, obtains revenue, and reinvests the revenue into infrastructure. This forms a completely different cycle: <strong>value returns to the investor itself — and the investor is the government</strong>.</p>
    <h3>6.3 Why China Can Build So Much Infrastructure</h3>
    <p>Over the past two decades: two-thirds of the world's high-speed rail in China, the world's largest subway network in China, thousands of bridges. Much of the funding comes from land lease revenue, land mortgage financing, local government financing vehicles. In essence: <strong>advance monetization of future appreciation</strong>.</p>
    <h3>6.4 The Value Capture Loop</h3>
    <p>Government invests in a subway → surrounding land appreciates → government leases usage rights → obtains revenue → builds the next subway. This is the land finance loop. From the GL Framework perspective, <strong>China is one of the few cases that truly forms a "cost and revenue return to the same system" loop</strong>.</p>
    <h3>6.5 Another Path for Administrative Friction</h3>
    <p>China has not only addressed land issues but also attempted to address administrative friction. The UK and US typically think in terms of adding procedures, adding regulation. China's approach in many places: if a process creates friction, remove the process. Digital payments, digital identity, digital government, facial recognition, one-stop online services. The core logic: do not measure waiting — eliminate waiting.</p>
    <h3>6.6 The Limits of Land Finance</h3>
    <p>Every loop has limits. The greatest risk of the China model is that land is not infinite, and population is not infinite. When population growth stops, real estate demand falls, land revenue falls, and the loop comes under pressure. The problem is not infrastructure itself. The problem is: <strong>short-term revenue supporting long-term assets</strong>.</p>
    <h3>6.7 Who Bears the Risk?</h3>
    <p>UK risk: renters bear it; the landless bear it. US risk: latecomers bear it; young people bear it. China risk: local finances bear it; future taxpayers bear it.</p>
    <h3>6.8 Summary of China's Ledger</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box">The China model attempts to answer a question that the UK and US have long left unanswered: <strong>how can the value created by public investment return to the public?</strong> The answer: state-owned land, land value capture, infrastructure reinvestment — forming a loop. This answer is not perfect, but it does make China one of the few systems in the world capable of large-scale land value capture and reinvestment.<br><br><strong>China's ledger in one sentence: the public bears the cost, value returns to the public and is reinvested into infrastructure; but long-term risks accumulate in local finances and future generations.</strong></div>

    <!-- Chapter 7: Global Commons -->
    <h2>Chapter Seven: The Global Commons and Wasted Land</h2>
    <h3>7.1 Land That Belongs to No One — Who Takes It?</h3>
    <p>The high seas, the deep seabed, Antarctica, outer space — legally, they are "the common heritage of mankind." But "mankind" is an entity with no voice, no organization, no bank account. The result: <strong>whoever gets there first, whoever has the capability, captures the value</strong>.</p>
    <h3>7.2 Summary</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box">At the global commons, the public (all of humanity) bears the cost, but the value is captured by <strong>those who arrive first, those with capability, those who occupy the advantaged positions</strong>. This is not a new logic — it is the cost-shifting logic described in Chapter One, playing out on a larger scale.</div>

    <!-- Chapter 8: Ghost GDP -->
    <h2>Chapter Eight: Ghost GDP</h2>
    <h3>8.1 Who Is Paying the Invisible Cost?</h3>
    <p>GDP records completed transactions. It does not record what is consumed in the transaction process — waiting, re-filing, giving up at a collapse point, psychological cost. Those costs do not disappear. They are shifted. They just are not on the ledger.</p>
    <h3>8.2 The Hotel Manager's Story</h3>
    <p>A hotel manager in Boston, earning about $4,000 a month after taxes. He found a studio — one open space with no separate bedroom — for about $2,500–3,000 per month. The agent told him: you need to pay first month's rent, last month's rent, and one month's security deposit. Total: about $7,500–9,000 upfront. He did not have it. He had to ask his parents for help. He moved to Quincy: rent $2,200–2,500, mandatory car ownership, parking fees. 64%–75% of his income disappears into rent and transportation.</p>
    <h3>8.3 Administrative Friction: The Hidden Tax of Institutional Design</h3>
    <p>The core insight of the GL Framework is: <strong>administrative friction is a tax</strong>. It is not a tax collected by the Ministry of Finance. It is a tax that institutional design extracts from the people. In the form of time, energy, psychological cost, opportunity cost.</p>
    <h3>8.4 Mental Health and Suicide</h3>
    <p>Boston Public Health Commission 2025 data: adult persistent anxiety rose from 21% to 26%; persistent sadness/hopelessness among high school students rose from 30% to 39%. Suicide is the most extreme form of cost shifting. GDP does not record these. GDP records funeral expenses, therapy hours, antidepressant prescriptions.</p>
    <h3>8.5 The Ghost GDP Formula</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box" style="text-align:center; font-family:monospace;">
        <strong>Ghost GDP = Σ (waiting cost + repetition cost + collapse cost + information asymmetry cost + psychological cost + opportunity cost)</strong>
    </div>
    <h3>8.6 Summary</h3>
    <div class="conclusion-box">GDP only sees completed transactions. It does not see who bears the cost. Ghost GDP is the shifted cost — never recorded on any national ledger.<br><br><strong>Capital can optimize what it can measure. It cannot optimize what it cannot see.</strong></div>

