    <h3>9.2 Returning to the Core Question</h3>
    <p>When the government spends a dollar to build a road, a subway, a high-speed rail line, a port, an airport — who ultimately captures the value? This question should not have a standard answer. What you need is: starting now, every time you see GDP grow, every time you see infrastructure completed, every time you sign a lease, ask yourself automatically: "<strong>Who captured the value? Who bore the shifted cost?</strong>"</p>
    <h3>9.3 Final Questions</h3>
    <p>If you live in the UK: how much of the land value increase was captured by aristocrats and the Crown?<br>
    If you live in the US: how much of the value created by public investment was captured by developers and capitalists?<br>
    If you live in Hong Kong: where did the land revenue go? Where do the developers' profits come from?<br>
    If you live in Taiwan: is the Land Value Increment Tax rate high enough? Why can't the younger generation afford housing?<br>
    If you live in China: where did the land lease revenue go? How large are the debt risks?<br>
    If you have ever signed a lease: where did those three months of rent go?<br>
    If you have ever doubted yourself in the middle of the night: you are not a failure. You are a person trying to survive in a system designed to consume you.</p>
    <p>No matter where you live, you can ask the same question: "<strong>In this country, in this city, in this market — how much does it cost to find a place to stand?</strong>" If you begin to ask these questions, this book has done its job.</p>

    <!-- Appendix -->
    <h2>Appendix: To Those Who Attack My Background</h2>
    <p>They attack me for "not being an economist," "lacking academic credentials," "having no government experience," "being Chinese," "being an amateur." But my background is not a defect — it is accumulation. Piano training gave me the ability to see complex systems and the ability to interpret symbols. The GL Framework did not grow out of any one country's land system. It grew out of the universal phenomenon of cost shifting. I use it to analyze the UK, the US, Hong Kong, Taiwan, China, the Global Commons — and the conclusion is that every system has its ledger. This is a mathematical result, not a political position. I am not here to compete with you. I am here to fill the gap you saw but did not know how to respond to. The old order will not die on its own. Someone has to dismantle it. This book is that act of dismantling.</p>
    
